=encoding utf8

=head1 NAME

std/internals - Runtime internals helpers for advanced modules.

=head1 SYNOPSIS

  from std/internals import class_name, object_slots, ansi_esc,
    ref_id, to_String, to_Number, to_Boolean, to_Regexp,
    make_instance, setprop, getprop, load_module;

  let obj_class := class_name(some_object);
  let slots := object_slots(some_object);
  let id := ref_id(some_object);

=head1 DESCRIPTION

This module provides low-level reflection helpers used by advanced
pure-Zuzu modules (for example C<std/dump>). These functions are
not intended for general application code.

Special properties are lexical metadata, not symbol bindings.
Calling C<setprop("paths", SomeClass)> does not create an identifier
named C<paths>.

=head1 EXPORTED FUNCTIONS

=over

=item * C<class_name(value)>

Returns the class name for object instances, else C<null>.

=item * C<object_slots(value)>

Returns a dictionary of object slots (excluding private underscore
prefixed slots), else C<null>.

=item * C<ansi_esc()>

Returns a one-character ANSI escape string.

=item * C<ref_id(value)>

Returns a stable reference id for reference-like values to support
cycle detection, else C<null>.

=item * C<to_String(value)>

Coerces C<value> using the same rules as string operators. Numbers are
stringified, booleans become C<"true"> or C<"false">, C<null> becomes
the empty string, regexps stringify to their pattern text, and objects
must provide C<to_String>. Values that string operators reject also
throw here.

=item * C<to_Number(value)>

Coerces C<value> using the same rules as numeric operators. Numeric
strings are parsed, booleans become C<1> or C<0>, C<null> becomes C<0>,
and objects must provide C<to_Number>. Values that numeric operators
reject also throw here.

=item * C<to_Boolean(value)>

Coerces C<value> using the same rules as logical operators, ternary
conditions, C<if>, and C<while>. C<null>, C<false>, C<0>, the empty
string, and empty collections are false. Objects with C<to_Boolean>
use that method and then boolify the result with the same rules.

=item * C<to_Regexp(value)>

Returns C<value> unchanged when it is already a regexp. Otherwise,
coerces C<value> with the string-operator rules and compiles the result
as a regexp pattern with no implicit flags. Invalid patterns throw.

=item * C<make_instance(klass, dict := null)>

Creates an instance of a user-defined class using the same field
defaults and field type checks as C<new>, but does not call
C<__build__>. When supplied, C<dict> provides slot values to install
after field defaults have been evaluated.

=item * C<load_module(module, symbol := null)>

Loads C<module> using the runtime's normal module loader. With only the
module name, returns a dictionary of the module's exports, including
underscore-prefixed names. With C<symbol>, returns only that exported
symbol. Missing modules or symbols throw an exception.

=item * C<setprop(key, value)>

Sets a lexically scoped runtime special property on the current frame.
Keys must be strings.

=item * C<getprop(key)>

Returns a lexically scoped runtime special property via outward lexical
lookup, else C<null>. Keys must be strings.

=back

=head1 EXAMPLES

  from std/internals import setprop, getprop;
  from std/path/z import ZPath;

  setprop( "paths", ZPath );
  say( getprop( "paths" ) );   // Class object

  do {
    setprop( "paths", null );
    say( getprop( "paths" ) ); // null (shadowed in this block)
  };

  say( getprop( "paths" ) );   // ZPath (outer lexical value)

